// Crusaders Mainframe — hal::IStore on the ATmega2560's 4 KB of EEPROM.
//
// WHY THERE IS NO INTEGRITY CHECK INSIDE THIS DRIVER
// --------------------------------------------------
// It would be one CRC byte per record and it is deliberately absent, because
// hal/Store.h puts the obligation on the CALLER: "callers must store an
// integrity check alongside the value and treat a mismatch as absent". A driver
// that quietly verified for them would be stricter than fakes::FakeStore — whose
// tearNextWriteAfter() hands back a mongrel value with kOk precisely because
// that is what real EEPROM does — and every caller's recovery path would then be
// tested against a danger the real device no longer presented. Two
// implementations of the same interface must not differ on what can go wrong.
//
// What IS checked here is STRUCTURE, which is a different thing: a torn write
// can leave a length byte reading 200, and honouring it would read past the end
// of the slot. An out-of-range length means the record is not a record, and it
// reads as kNotFound.
//
// LAYOUT
// ------
// Six fixed slots, matching the six the fake offers, sized for a device id, a
// bridge token, an SSID, a password, a court map and one spare so kNoSpace is
// reachable in a test. 306 bytes of the 4096 available; the rest is left alone
// because nothing else in this system has a claim on it yet and a layout that
// spans the whole EEPROM cannot be extended without moving every record.
//
// WRITE ORDER
// -----------
// The occupancy marker goes down FIRST and the value last. The other order
// leaves a slot holding a complete-looking record whose bytes were never
// written; this order leaves one that is visibly occupied and whose contents
// the caller's own integrity check will reject.
//
